description:
StageUpdateParamsSchema in packages/agent-protocol/src/index.ts (lines 253-262) declares four top-level fields: `colorEffect`, `blendMode`, `loopMode`, and `firstFrame`. However, the `stage_update` handler in packages/authoring-ui/src/agent/registry.ts (lines 940-955) only forwards `params.updates` to `updateDisplayObject` — it never reads or merges these four top-level fields. Any MCP client that sends:

  { id: '...', colorEffect: { type: 'tint', ... } }

will receive an ok response but the colorEffect will NOT be applied to the display object. The schema actively misleads callers into thinking these are distinct named parameters.

Root cause: the schema was added (task 1046) to make the fields discoverable, but the registry handler was never updated to actually use them.

Fix: in the registry `stage_update` handler, merge the top-level params into the updates object before calling `updateDisplayObject`. For example:
  const merged = { ...params.updates };
  if (params.colorEffect !== undefined) merged.colorEffect = params.colorEffect;
  if (params.blendMode !== undefined) merged.blendMode = params.blendMode;
  if (params.loopMode !== undefined) merged.loopMode = params.loopMode;
  if (params.firstFrame !== undefined) merged.firstFrame = params.firstFrame;

Also update the `DisplayObjectUpdates` type in packages/core/src/model/timeline.ts to include `colorEffect`, `blendMode`, `loopMode`, and `firstFrame` so TypeScript does not strip these at the function boundary.

Acceptance: calling `stage_update` with a top-level `colorEffect: { type: 'tint', tint: '#ff0000', tintPercent: 100 }` on a SymbolInstance results in the colorEffect being persisted in the document model and visible in the published SWF.
